Year = {1997},
Volume = {36},
Number = {6},
Pages = {152+},
Month = {JUN},
Abstract = {APPLICATIONS for wireless systems continue to explode and fill the
   airwaves with signals at 900, 1800, and 2400 MHz. The `'wireless
   revolution'' is not limited to these relatively-low frequencies,
   however. Once considered the sole domain of military applications, such
   as missile-guidance systems, millimeter-wave frequencies including 38,
   60, and 77 GHz are finding favor among designers of wireless systems.
   Once restricted to military and local-government use, these and other
   bands rep resent a great deal of promise and potential for wireless
   markets. With the growth of commercial applications into millimeter-wave
   frequencies the need arises for higher-frequency vector network
   analyzers (VNAs) that are capable of providing accurate magnitude and
   phase measurements at those frequencies of interest. The answer may lie
   with a new 1-mm coaxial-connector-based broadband VNA system from
   Hewlett-Packard Co. (Santa Rosa, CA), the model HP 8510XF. This advanced
   VNA is capable of performing a continuous frequency sweep from 45 MHz to
   110 GHz while capturing S-parameter data on active and passive devices
   and components.},
