@inproceedings{ WOS:000308771500016,
Author = {Robert, Jean and Serra, Jean-Jacques and Jung, Philippe},
Editor = {Freeman, M},
Title = {Maruca: An Early French Liquid-Fueled Rocket},
Booktitle = {HISTORY OF ROCKETRY AND ASTRONAUTICS},
Series = {AAS History Series},
Year = {2012},
Volume = {37},
Pages = {287+},
Note = {40th History Symposium of the International-Academy-of-Astronautics,
   Valencia, SPAIN, 2006},
Organization = {Int Acad Astronaut},
Abstract = {While early French Army rocket activities have for some time been
   well-documented, and lately the significant Air Ministry ones, it only
   recently came to the surface that the French Navy also was extremely
   active with missiles, being second only to the aeronautical efforts.
   Like the other French arms, even before the signature of the armistice,
   La Marine went in to Germany scouring for German missiles. It created a
   German Secret Weapons Commission in October 1944, with a team based in
   Constance. A GANES group was then established one year later, with the
   goal of analyzing German hardware, and using it as a basis for
   developing new weapons, mainly flying torpedoes and surface-to-air
   missiles.
   While activities on the German FX and Hs 293 flying bombs did not
   proceed further than limited reverse-engineering production, the Navy's
   DCCAN Engins Speciaux group, led by Jean-Jacques Peyrat, was more
   interested in the information the latter had brought from Germany about
   the Hs 117 Schmetterling, as the decision to develop a surface-to-air
   missile was made in 1948.
   No Schmetterling was ever launched from France, but this missile was
   significantly redesigned under the name of MARUCA (MArine RUelle Contre
   Avion-against plane). Flight testing started in 1951, with early
   versions keeping the same general shape and guidance system. The
   sustainer however was replaced by a new one, keeping nitric acid, but
   with Furaline instead of Tonka, while boosters were French-made.
   Problems with the latter's separation led to different configurations,
   Al to A5. In the fall of 1954, a much modified A6 version, of canard
   configuration, was developed in order to increase the flying speed of
   the missile, from Mach 0.72 to 0.85. It later was tested with a new
   guidance system, using a modified naval fire control radar. From 1959,
   several MARUCA A6's were launched from the Ile d'Oleron research ship,
   confirming that a liquidfueled missile was not practical for operational
   naval use.
   For this reason, as soon as June 1953, the ES group had considered
   developing a solid-fueled supersonic version under the name MARUCA C,
   which later became the MASURCA (MArine SUpersonic Ruelle Contre Avions).
   The last MARUCA accordingly were used as test beds for the MASURCA
   program until 1961.
   This paper, with a wealth of completely new information, provides the
   most complete description currently available of the MARUCA program,
   from its inception in 1948 until the final 1961 flights. It will present
   a technical description of each version, as well as an outline of the
   guidance research conducted during a program which saw no less than 200
   missiles built.},
