   21-23, 2025},
Organization = {Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc},
Abstract = {The Global Navigation Satellite System (GNSS) has become an integral
   part of daily life worldwide, supporting applications in navigation,
   time synchronization, the financial sector, power generation, taxi
   services, and even food distribution networks. The Navigation with
   Indian Constellation (NavIC), a regional satellite navigation system
   developed by the Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO), enhances
   GNSS capabilities, particularly over the Indian subcontinent. ISRO has
   also developed the PSLV Orbital Platform Experiment (POEM), which
   repurposes the spent upper stage of its launch vehicle as a stabilized
   platform for conducting microgravity experiments in low Earth orbit.
   This platform is equipped with a high-precision, dual-constellation GNSS
   receiver to provide accurate positioning in orbit. This paper presents
   the design and applications of a dual-constellation GNSS receiver
   intended for diverse orbital environments beyond standard positioning.
   The receiver's primary function is navigation, supporting 12 GPS
   channels, 7 NavIC channels, and 2 GAGAN channels to deliver robust
   navigation data. Its secondary function is precise timing, where it
   operates as a master clock, offering accurate Universal Time (UT) for
   payload operations. The third application involves ionospheric
   characterization-specifically profiling the plasmasphere above 350 km
   altitude over the Indian region using dual-frequency NavIC signals. The
   fourth application is NavIC-based telecommanding, which enables
   ground-based command of onboard systems through NavIC satellite links.
   This paper details the GNSS receiver's hardware architecture, software
   design, and on-orbit performance.},
