@inproceedings{ WOS:000178200800223,
Author = {Soper, D and Heim, PK and Freeman, R},
Book-Group-Author = {MARINE TECHNOLOGY SOCIETY
   MARINE TECHNOLOGY SOCIETY
   MARINE TECHNOLOGY SOCIETY},
Title = {US navy issues in electronic navigation: Transitioning from
   ``traditional{''} to ``digital navigation{''}},
Booktitle = {OCEANS 2001 MTS/IEEE: AN OCEAN ODYSSEY, VOLS 1-4, CONFERENCE PROCEEDINGS},
Year = {2001},
Pages = {1441-1445},
Note = {Annual Conference of the Marine-Technology-Society, HONOLULU, HI, NOV
   05-08, 2001},
Organization = {Marine Technol Soc; IEEE; OES; Minerals, Met \& Mat Soc; Soc Explorat
   Geophysicists; Amer Geophys Union; Womens Aquat Network; Coasts, Oceans,
   Ports \& Rivers Inst; Amer Meteorol Soc; Oceanog Soc},
Abstract = {The United States Navy will be faced with numerous challenges while
   transitioning from centuries of manually plotting and navigating using
   paper charts to electronic charts with automated functions and displays.
   Training and education will be essential to minimizing the challenges
   and reducing the effects of potential obstacles. It is anticipated that
   new technology and capabilities will enable the Navy to conduct
   operations more effectively, more efficiently and with increased margins
   of safety.
   The transition will require numerous paradigm shifts and will involve
   many different Navy activities. The transition will require coordination
   among resource sponsors, systems commands, training commands, Fleet
   Commander-in-Chiefs (CINC's), Type Commanders, Numbered Fleet
   Commanders, all the way down to the unit level. Given the massive amount
   of coordination required, the Chief of Naval Operations (CNO) formally
   designated the Oceanographer of the Navy (CNO N096, RADM Dick West) as
   Navigator of the Navy. Coincident with this designation, the original
   Navy goal of achieving a ``paperless bridge{''} by 2007 has been
   accelerated to 2004. To test this concept, two Navy Carrier Battle
   Groups (CVBG's) will deploy with certified Electronic Chart Display and
   Information System-Navy (ECDIS-N) systems in 2002.
   As the Navy conducts this transition, there is a need for an overarching
   strategy and policy guidance for fleet units. The Chief of Naval
   Operations issues the ECDIS-N Policy, system certification procedures,
   and guidance on Geospatial Information and Services (GI\&S). Fleet
   CINC's and Type Commanders will provide guidance on crew training and
   unit level certification.
   The Navigator of the Navy's vision (10+ years from now) is for a
   virtual, digital 4-dimensional cube of the environment. The four
   dimensions being three-dimensional space (x,y,z) plus the fourth
   dimension, time. Layers of additional valuable, information are imbedded
   within the cube; digital information such as timely and accurate
   positions of friendly and enemy forces, threat envelopes of enemy radars
   and missile batteries, meteorology and oceanography data, etc. This 4-D
   cube will extend from the bottom of the ocean to the top of the
   atmosphere and will be available to all services (Army, Navy, Air Force,
   Marines). Accurate positional GI\&S information will be the foundation
   on which this 4-Cube is based and will be absolutely critical to focused
   integration of forces for synergistic effects essential to the success
   of any Service, Joint, or Coalition exercise, mission or operation.
   With the advent of NAVSTAR Global Positioning System (GPS), mariners are
   now capable of navigating ships with much greater accuracy than ever
   before. However, there are inherent limitations to paper nautical charts
   when plotting GPS positions. Knowing the datum of the chart on which GPS
   positions are plotted is critical to ensuring positional accuracy with
   respect to charted hazards. In many circumstances the plotted position
   of certain printed features on paper charts is less accurate than the
   GPS position of the ship being plotted.
   With the advent of electronic navigation systems, plotting errors are
   minimized. The National Imagery and Mapping Agency's (NIMA) Digital
   Nautical Charts (DNCs(R)) and the International Hydrographic
   Organization (IHO) Standard S-57 format Electronic Navigation Charts
   (ENCs) are plotted using the world recognized standard WGS-84 datum, the
   same datum on which GPS Is based. Electronic charting systems provide
   mariners with real-time information about where they ARE as opposed to
   where they WERE. With automation of many of the plotting and chart
   maintenance procedures that were previously done manually, mariners can
   now spend more time evaluating their situation rather than plotting
   their position or manually updating a chart.
   Electronic Chart Display and Information Systems (ECDIS) whether on
   civilian or military surface ships, rely extensively on GPS for
   positional input. Submarine ECDIS-N rely on inertial navigation systems
   for inputs with occasional inputs from GPS. However, since GPS may be
   jammed or potentially become unusable, reliable back-up navigational
   Inputs (i.e., ship's position, course and speed) must be made available
   to the electronic charting system from some other reliable source.
   Inertial, Doppler, celestial, bathymetric, geophysical or some other
   source must be available to ``drive{''} the system position. In
   addition, inputs such as visual bearings, radar bearing and ranges,
   celestial Lines of Position (LOP's) and fixes must be readily accepted
   by the system.},
