@article{ WOS:001661882100002,
Author = {Hou, Xing'an and Su, Shi and Fan, Yihong and Yang, Mingwei and Qin,
   Yihao and Liu, Jinsheng and Fang, Chunshuai},
Title = {High Magnitude Simulation and Calibration Technique of Single Star
   Simulator},
Journal = {ACTA OPTICA SINICA},
Year = {2025},
Volume = {45},
Number = {24},
Month = {DEC},
Abstract = {Objective Based on the stars in the field of view, the missile-borne
   star sensor corrects the errors of the missile-borne inertial navigation
   system and forms an integrated missile-borne inertial/astronomical
   navigation system to improve the navigation accuracy of the missile. At
   the same time, higher requirements are put forward for the simulation of
   high-magnitude and high-precision target stars using a star simulator
   for calibration. It is found that the light and dark contrast of the
   light source, the dimming ratio of the system and the stability of the
   system are the important factors that affect the simulation of
   high-magnitude and high-precision. Therefore, aiming at the problems of
   low simulation accuracy and difficult calibration of high-magnitude and
   high-precision, we take the standardized static single star simulator as
   an example to study the simulation of high-magnitude and high-precision.
   Methods A high-magnitude simulation system consisting of an light
   emitting diode (LED) light source, an integrating sphere, a
   photodetector, a mechanical optical fiber attenuator and a
   transmission-type parallel light tube is designed. Firstly, the
   relationship among photodetector reading, attenuation coefficient of the
   optical attenuator and outgoing illumination is derived by combining the
   image plane illumination formula and Gaussian beam theory, and the
   stellar magnitude energy model of the system is established. Then, the
   operating principle of the pulse width modulation (PWM) light source
   dimming method and the mechanical optical fiber attenuator dimming
   method are analyzed respectively, and a light block with an aperture
   that varies with rotation angle is designed as the attenuation stop of
   the mechanical optical fiber attenuator. Finally, through theoretical
   error analysis and experimental validation, it is verified that the
   magnitude simulation system can be calibrated directly by the optical
   power of the photodetector and the attenuation coefficient of the
   optical attenuator, which can realize the simulation of high-magnitude
   and high-precision. Results and Discussions Based on the image plane
   illuminance formula, Gaussian beam theory and static single-star
   simulator, the magnitude energy model is established, and the system
   magnitude calculation formula (equation 9) is obtained. The
   stress-strain, vibration mode analysis and attenuation performance
   evaluation of the designed water-drop-type attenuating diaphragm are
   carried out. The test results show that the attenuation error of the
   designed optical attenuator is approximately within +/- 0.5 degrees o in
   a certain attenuation range. Based on the magnitude calculation formula
   and the preceding accuracy analysis, we conduct a calibration experiment
   to verify the feasibility of evaluating the magnitude simulation
   accuracy using the photodetector's optical power and the optical
   attenuator's attenuation coefficient. The system achieves a continuously
   adjustable magnitude range from 0 to 12 Mv. The magnitude simulation
   accuracy is better than 0.2 Mv, and the stability is better than 3\%.
   Conclusions This paper introduces a closed-loop light source control
   system for a standard single-star simulator, comprising an LED light
   source, an integrating sphere, a photodetector, and a mechanical optical
   fiber attenuator. A droplet-type attenuating diaphragm, with an aperture
   that varies with rotation angle, is designed. The attenuation
   repeatability error of the optical attenuator is less than 0.5 \%.
   Through the verification of photodetector optical power and optical
   attenuator attenuation coefficient, the system achieves a wide range of
   dynamic magnitude adjustment from 0 to 12 Mv, with a magnitude
   simulation accuracy better than 0.2 Mv, and stability better than 3 \%.
   The results demonstrate that the attenuation accuracy of high-magnitude
   analog attenuators is a major source of error. The subsequent work will
   be optimized around the design of the attenuator curve, the control
   algorithm of the optical attenuator and the scalability of the light
   source to improve the simulation ability of the real weak target.},
