   Div; Univ Pittsburgh},
Abstract = {The advent of optical fiber revolutionized the way the world exchanges
   and processes information. The benefits of this technology have
   manifested themselves in many advancements in business and science. This
   is why it is important that students become familiar with optical
   fibers, how they work, and how they have been so widely integrated in
   existing real world applications. An undergraduate laboratory experiment
   was designed by a senior electrical engineering student. First, students
   learn about the physics of fibers and investigate different properties
   of multi-mode and single mode fibers. The second goal is to apply this
   knowledge to a functional fiber sensor in the laboratory. The experiment
   is part of the laboratory component of an optoelectronics elective
   course, EE341 `'Optoelectronic Materials and Devices'', offered in the
   Electrical Engineering Department at Bucknell University. {[}1]
   Communications is probably the most well known application of optical
   fiber. However, optical fibers are also used in fiber sensors for
   applications such as automotive sensors, structural sensors, avionics,
   and inertial guidance systems for missiles. These sensors offer
   advantages such as light weight, compactness, and EMI (Electro-Magnetic
   Interference) immunity. This undergraduate experiment focuses on such
   sensors and demonstrates the interdisciplinary appeal of optoelectronics
   which integrates knowledge from fields including physics, electrical
   engineering, materials science, and chemistry. This approach represents
   an effort to convey the significance of optical fiber in real world
   applications to undergraduate students. The lab effectively complements
   an existing laboratory experiment where students create a communication
   link. {[}2]
   Investigation of various types of optical fibers and their properties
   led to the development of an experimental procedure in which students
   study properties of multimode fiber and a single mode interferometric
   fiber sensor. In addition to acquiring knowledge about fiber and fiber
   sensors, the senior design student also gained experience in designing a
   laboratory experiment and effectively communicating with lab students.},
