   HOPKINS UNIV, APPL PHYS LAB, LAUREL, MD, APR 16-19, 1996},
Organization = {Int Acad Astronaut; Ctr Natl Etud Spatiales; Deut Agenur
   Raumfahrtangelegenheiten; European Space Agcy; Inst Space \& Astronaut
   Sci; NASA; Planetary Soc; Space Res Inst},
Abstract = {The development of a new family of launch vehicles by Lockheed Martin to
   support the current trend toward smaller, rapidly developed, cheaper
   spacecraft programs is described. An overview of the Lockheed Martin
   Launch Vehicle (LMLV) family of launch vehicles is provided, with
   specific discussion of the propulsion, avionics, structures, payload
   options, and payload adapter and separation system options. LMLV's use
   of Thiokol's Star motors as injection stages is presented for mission
   requirements other than low Earth orbit. Typical launch environments at
   the spacecraft interface are identified, and the LMLV Program's special
   attention to reducing launch operations costs is presented through a
   discussion of LMLV's `'stack-and-shoot'' parallel-processing
   methodology; providing minimum interaction between the LMLV and
   spacecraft launch processing timelines. An overview of the standard LMLV
   launch services is presented, with discussion of the launch services
   flow, payload processing, and launch vehicle processing. The results of
   the first LMLV1 launch in August of 1995 are summarized, along with the
   current LMLV contract manifest. Copyright (C) 1997 Published by Elsevier
   Science Ltd.},
