Title = {NASA'S Sounding Rocket Program NSROC, recovery systems and Mesquito
   vehicle development},
Booktitle = {18TH ESA SYMPOSIUM ON EUROPEAN ROCKET AND BALLOON PROGRAMMES AND RELATED
   RESEARCH},
Series = {ESA Special Publications},
Year = {2007},
Volume = {647},
Pages = {111-116},
Note = {18th ESA Symposium on European Rocket and Balloon Programmes and Related
   Research, Visby, SWEDEN, JUN 03-07, 2007},
Organization = {European Space Agcy},
Abstract = {The NSROC Program (NASA Sounding Rocket Operations) was privatized in
   February 1999 at NASA Wallops Island, Virginia, USA. NSROC Teammates
   Northrop Grumman and Orbital Sciences Corporation continue to
   successfully implement the NASA Sounding Rocket Program. NSROC provides
   the cradle-to-grave program for the worldwide scientific community. As
   of 15 February 2007, NSROC has performed 181 Sounding Rocket flights at
   launch ranges around the globe. These missions have supported various
   science disciplines including Geospace, Solar/Heliospheric, High Energy
   Astrophysics, UV/Optical Astrophysics, Solar System Exploration, Student
   Outreach and Technology Innovation.
   Recovery of sounding rocket payloads is typical for many of the NSROC
   launches at both land and coastal launch ranges. A payload's reentry
   characteristics, thermal distribution and dynamic stability though the
   atmosphere are critical for the proper positioning and preparation for
   the recovery phase. A certain Black Brant IX mission from White Sands
   Missile Range in the summer of 2006 noted peculiar reentry dynamics that
   led to an atypical recovery. Details of this mission and its recovery
   will be shared.
   NSROC is developing the Mesquito sounding rocket vehicle for lower
   mesosphere scientific investigations. The two-stage vehicle includes a
   high thrust, short duration first stage motor and a non-propulsive
   second stage that also allows for rapid reload capability. The
   streamlined processing allows for the ability to launch a series of
   vehicles that can provide both temporal and spatial measurements and is
   planned for 30 minute interval launches. The Mesquito is a low cost
   sounding rocket that can contain a user specified payload or can
   interface with a small suite of avionics and instrument(s). A full
   telemetry suite is available to transmit 2 mbit/sec of payload data. The
   data can include analog, digital, parallel, synchronous, asynchronous
   and/or video data. System details and the qualification status will be
   shared.},
