   SEP 20-21, 2007},
Abstract = {The H-II Transfer Vehicle (HTV) is a Japanese unmanned cargo transfer
   vehicle developed for the International Space Station (ISS) re-supply
   and waste disposal purposes, and will be launched on a new Japanese
   launch vehicle H-IIB in the summer of 2009.
   The HTV will play an important role in the ISS logistics planning
   especially after the Shuttle retirement in 2010. The HTV consists of
   four major modules, i.e., the avionics, propulsion, pressurized
   logistics carrier and unpressurized logistic carrier modules. After each
   module completed the protoflight test (PFT) at the contractor site, they
   will be delivered and integrated in Tsulcuba for the final system
   integration tests.
   This paper presents the overview and the latest status of the HTV
   development in general, and then focuses on the avionics module, which
   contains the primary part of the HTV avionics system, including the
   navigation, guidance and control subsystem (GNC). We recently completed
   the GNC flight software verification and subsystem tests, and the module
   is currently undergoing the module level integration tests, prior to the
   shipping to Tsukuba for further integration. This paper presents the
   results from those GNC flight software and subsystem tests as well.},
