@inproceedings{ WOS:000072955800069,
Author = {Baker, RL and Richardson, JF},
Editor = {Hoots, FR and Kaufman, B and Cefola, PJ and Spencer, DB},
Title = {Space Lift Range System launch vehicle trajectory measurement capability
   Trade Study Results},
Booktitle = {ASTRODYNAMICS 1997},
Series = {ADVANCES IN THE ASTRONAUTICAL SCIENCES},
Year = {1998},
Volume = {97},
Number = {1-2},
Pages = {1099-1118},
Note = {AAS/AIAA Astrodynamics Conference, SUN VALLEY, ID, AUG 04-07, 1997},
Organization = {Amer Astronaut Soc; Amer Inst Aeronaut \& Astronaut},
Abstract = {New Eastern and Western Range metric tracking capabilities are to be
   implemented for the Air Force Space Command under the Range
   Standardization and Automation (RSA) Program and associated efforts. A
   technical assessment and Life Cycle Cost (LCC) evaluation of the
   proposed use of GPS based instrumentation compared to conventional radar
   based instrumentation for space lift launch vehicle (LV) trajectory
   position and velocity determination has been completed and documented in
   the GPS Trade Study Report. This was the first step in development of
   the Space Lift Range System (SLRS) metric architecture. This paper
   provides an overview of the GPS Trade Study results.
   The study concludes that GPS provides the most cost effective and
   technically feasible method of acquiring LV trajectory data. GPS
   capabilities provide generally improved accuracy and significant cost
   savings compared to metric radar. These benefits are very substantial
   even when redundant ground and launch vehicle GPS instrumentation are
   specified. Payback in savings from eliminated radars occurs in four
   years or less after transitioning if radar modernization that would
   otherwise be needed is considered. Savings are maximized by
   transitioning as soon as possible, but budget and practical constraints
   may cause the transition period to be on the order of six to seven
   years.
   The study provides top le;el GPS support data flow diagrams and
   identifies Eastern and Western Range radar affected by the transition to
   GPS for trajectory data. Radar used for debris track and space object
   tracking requirements are retained and upgraded to improve reliability
   and operating costs. Accuracy and reliability requirements used for the
   study were those provided in the SLRS Technical Requirements Document
   (TRD).
   Accurate ranging signals from the GPS Satellite Constellation are
   essentially uniform over the globe and highly reliable. The Government
   is fully committed to maintaining GPS as a national asset, therefore
   developing a range architecture that takes full advantage of it ensures
   adequate metric capabilities at the lowest overall cost. Also of
   significant economic advantage is the tremendous technology drive due to
   the large and rapidly expanding GPS user equipment market, energized by
   the many military and civilian applications.
   Ground implementation of a metric architecture to support onboard GPS
   receivers is based on standard telemetry instrumentation and data
   processing which is readily adaptable to support automation and remote
   control. This allows for minimum staffing and fast response.
   The SLRS architecture will incorporate the GPS translator signal
   processing capabilities provided at the Eastern Range to support the
   Navy Trident Program. At this time, additional radar support or GPS
   instrumentation are being considered for tracking the SRBs for the NASA
   Shuttle Program. The Air Force Minuteman ICBM Program is currently
   considering GPS instrumentation for future support.
   Development of the EELV and the X-33 may change the need for the current
   medium and heavy LVs to perform GPS integration and flight
   certification. Light vehicles (i.e. Pegasus) will transition to GPS.
   Recommendations are provided for support of the Ranges' current and
   anticipated programs. A timeline of key milestones and decision points
   is provided which takes into account expected influences by
   demonstration of new technologies and by inclusion of new LV programs.},
