Title = {SPIRIT: Student projects involving rocket investigation techniques},
Booktitle = {14TH ESA SYMPOSIUM ON EUROPEAN ROCKET AND BALLOON PROGRAMMES AND RELATED
   RESEARCH},
Series = {ESA SPECIAL PUBLICATIONS},
Year = {1999},
Volume = {437},
Pages = {593-598},
Note = {14th ESA Symposium on European Rocket and Balloon Programmes and Related
   Research, POTSDAM, GERMANY, MAY 31-JUN 03, 1999},
Organization = {European Space Agency; German Aerosp Ctr},
Abstract = {In 1997, our university in collaboration with two other institutions
   (The State University of New York (SUNY) - Geneseo and Lincoln
   University) was awarded a sounding rocket flight opportunity supported
   by NASA through its Student Launch Program. This relatively unique award
   was the outcome of competition based on the educational, as well as
   scientific, merits of the proposal. Our proposed program, entitled
   ``SPIRIT{''} (Student Projects Involving Rocket Investigation
   Techniques), provides a unique opportunity to accomplish meaningful
   science and instrumentation development in an educational forum. The
   science theme for SPIRIT is the measurement of mesospheric temperature
   and dynamic behavior utilizing novel sensor techniques. Students will
   test and compare five methods for obtaining these data. They will use
   two instrumented falling spheres, Langmuir probes, a small mass
   spectrometer, and an optical measurement of the rotational/vibrational
   structure of OH. In an advance over present methods, the falling spheres
   (one rigid and one inflated) will be instrumented with accelerometers
   and telemetry. This will significantly improve the measurement
   technique, saving in future sounding rockets the high costs and
   considerable logistics involved in providing a high-precision radar.
   The faculty and students at the three universities communicate by
   e-mail, telephone, videoconferences, and joint trips to NASA Wallops
   Flight Facility for meetings such as the Project Initiation Conference
   and the Experimenters' Design Review. The project is organized into
   teams of 7-10 students for scientific experiments, structure, telemetry,
   power and wiring, management, and communications and outreach. The
   students range from first-year to graduate, but the majority are lower
   division who are maturing with the project. They come from many
   different academic disciplines. Most of them meet weekly in an
   interdisciplinary class established for the project. This is a very
   useful way of keeping everyone up-to-date and on-task, as well as for
   developing personal relationships. The class is also the main mechanism
   for the accomplishments of SPIRIT to date-the science issues under
   consideration, the new sensor technology developed for measuring
   temperature, the educational and outreach activities, and the assessment
   of the program on the students' education.},
