Note = {AIAA SciTech Forum, Orlando, FL, JAN 08-12, 2024},
Organization = {AIAA},
Abstract = {On November 16th, 2022, NASA launched the inaugural test flight of the
   Space Launch System (SLS) carrying the Orion capsule into a high orbit
   far beyond the Moon. The launch vehicle was instrumented with over three
   thousand flight instrumentation sensors, many of which monitored
   aerodynamic, acoustic, structural, and thermal environments. These data
   are intended contribute to the validation of experimental and numerical
   tools used to predict the design environments that the vehicle
   experiences during launch and ascent. Prior to launch, tests were
   performed at the SLS Systems Integration Laboratory (SIL) using
   flightlike avionics and on the integrated flight hardware of Artemis I
   at the Vehicle Assembly Building (VAB). The purpose of these tests was
   to characterize the data acquisition units (DAUs) used to record and
   telemeter flight data to ground stations in order to assure that flight
   test objectives can be achieved and to quantify the expected quality of
   the flight data. In addition, preflight assessment and development of
   tools and methods used to process and disseminate flight data at the
   Huntsville Operations Support Center (HOSC) were conducted and
   adjustments made with respect to DAU time synchronization prior to and
   after the flight. This paper summarizes these tests. Some aspects of the
   data postprocessing are discussed.},
