Year = {2017},
Volume = {47},
Pages = {207-218},
Note = {49th History Symposium of the International-Academy-of-Astronautics,
   Jerusalem, ISRAEL, OCT, 2015},
Abstract = {During the 1960s, a fascinating, hands-on approach was introduced to the
   Technion Israel's technology institute student-built rockets. The aim of
   this chapter is to describe the activities of rocketry as an educational
   and research tool at the Technion in the 1970s. From a modest beginning
   in the late 1960s, the faculty for aeronautics used to build
   student-research rockets, as a hands-on project. The rockets became
   bigger and more sophisticated, and many new features were included such
   as telemetry, avionics, and multi-staging. The rockets of the Technion
   were designed by faculty staff and students, and some assistance in the
   field of casting solid propellants was provided by the Israeli defense
   industries, and the military gave permission to use its test ranges. The
   small rockets were a very humble beginning of Israel's quest for space,
   which culminated decades later, when Israel lofted its satellite to
   space by its indigenous designed Shavit (Comet) launch vehicle. This
   chapter is the second in a series of articles by the author, the first
   presented at the 65th International Astronautical Congress
   (IAC-14-E4.2.5),(1) tracking the research rockets projects conducted in
   Israel since the 1960s to the late 1990s and beyond.},
