Title = {Angular Rate Estimator for LEO Microsatellite Application to Future
   Algerian Microsatellite},
Booktitle = {RAST 2009: PROCEEDINGS OF THE 4TH INTERNATIONAL CONFERENCE ON RECENT
   ADVANCES IN SPACE TECHNOLOGIES},
Year = {2009},
Pages = {573-578},
Note = {4th International Conference on Recent Advances in Space Technologies,
   Istanbul, TURKEY, JUN 11-13, 2009},
Organization = {Turkish Air Force Acad; Istanbul Tech Univ; Bogazici Univ; Marmara Univ;
   Bahcesehir Univ; Istanbul Commerce Univ; Halic Univ; Yeditepe Univ;
   IEEE; AIAA},
Abstract = {After separation of future Algerian microsatellite from the launch
   vehicle, the satellite can be tumbling at an unknown rate (up to a
   maximum of 36 degrees/second in any axis e.g. Alsat-1 the first Algerian
   microsatellite). In such a phase, it can be difficult to get the full
   attitude knowledge of microsatellite, however it can be possible to
   determine coarse angular velocity vector of the microsatellite using the
   difference measurement of two successive magnetometer readings. It must
   be useful to put Alsat-2 attitude from the unknown random attitude to a
   predictable attitude. Assuming that future Algerian microsatellite is 3
   axis controlled, agile off-pointing and its attitude determination and
   control system uses X/Y/Z reaction wheels, 3-axis magnetorquer rods and
   single butane gas thruster for orbital maintenance. The attitude sensor
   used, is a 3-axis fluxgate magnetometer, sun sensor, gyroscope and star
   sensor. Precise orbital knowledge will be obtained using an antenna GPS
   receiver supported by an on-board orbit estimator. This paper describes
   the various steps to design the rate kalman filter: (1) System equation
   modeling, (2) Process and observation noise matrix, (3) Observation
   equation modeling. Simulation results will be presented to evaluate the
   performance of the filter and finally the conclusion.},
