Abstract = {The United States Military Academy's SPace Engineering and Applied
   Research (SPEAR) Tactical Hypersonic Orbital Rocket (THOR) program is an
   interdisciplinary capstone project that educates and inspires senior
   cadets through the design and launch of two-stage solid rockets. Since
   its inception in 2018, the program has sought to cross the Karman Line -
   altitudes above 100km. Building on the successes of previous years, the
   Academic Year 2025 SPEAR-THOR successfully designed and launched four,
   two-stage solid rockets at speeds exceeding Mach 6.2 and altitudes of
   149km.
   This paper presents the simulation architecture, structural design
   methodology, and payload configuration developed by the team. Notable
   contributions include the integration of Missile Lab, Missile DATCOM,
   and Aero3D to model flight dynamics, and the redesign of the payload bay
   to improve structural integrity and reduce telemetry interference.
   Flight data and postlaunch analysis confirm that the SPEAR-THOR rocket
   can reliably and predictably reach space at hypersonic speeds using
   low-cost materials and student-led engineering.},
