@inproceedings{ WOS:000370294400150,
Author = {Cohen, Raphael and Anh-Toan Bui Long and Jobredeaux, Romain and Feron,
   Eric},
Book-Group-Author = {IEEE},
Title = {A CREDIBLE AUTOCODING APPLICATION WITHIN A ROCKET AND ITS PAYLOAD},
Booktitle = {2015 IEEE/AIAA 34TH DIGITAL AVIONICS SYSTEMS CONFERENCE (DASC)},
Series = {IEEE-AIAA Digital Avionics Systems Conference},
Year = {2015},
Note = {IEEE/AIAA 34th Digital Avionics Systems Conference (DASC), Prague, CZECH
   REPUBLIC, SEP 13-17, 2015},
Organization = {IEEE; AIAA; AESS; AIAA Digital Avion Tech Comm; Boeing; Honeywell;
   MITRE; Mosaic Atm; NASA; Avion Magazine},
Abstract = {The cost of software engineering and avionics represents more than half
   of the budget of a safety-critical system like an aircraft or a UAV. The
   quantity and the complexity of embedded software in such systems is
   increasing exponentially. Reducing their cost of development is a major
   objective in the aeronautics and avionics industry. Research in Cyber
   Physical Systems (CPS) endeavours to contribute to this objective. The
   traditionnal approach to certifying software is to design unit tests to
   match low level requirements, and integration tests to validate high
   level requirements. Creating and running these tests require large
   amounts of time and money. With the recent bloom of companies
   specialized in UAV and small systems design, there is a need for new
   methods able to generate reliable codes in large projects easily,
   quickly, and in a cost efficient manner. Formal methods are a collection
   of techniques for the mathematical analysis of programs and models,
   which can assist in the certification of software with respect to FAA
   and EASA certification guidelines.
   New approaches to software verification, using formal methods, have been
   developed in the past few years. In this article, we will focus on a
   particular method of code generation called the Credible Autocoding
   {[}1]. Credible Autocoding is a method of certification rooted in
   model-based design principles and credible compilation. In particular,
   the tool described in {[}2] accepts as an input a Simulink model of a
   system and then translates it into annotated C code. Simulink is widely
   used by control engineers, and this technique enables the design to
   occur at this high level of description, while guarantees are given on
   the C implementation. C code is generated containing a mathematical
   proof of desirable properties of the system in annotations. The
   annotations, included in the C code as comments, describe the behavior
   of the system in a formal language. Using proper formal analysis tools,
   the validity of these comments can be assessed {[}3].
   We demonstrate an experimental implementation of this technique on a
   system. We will present an application on a rocket system and its
   payload. We used Credible Autocoding for the control command of the
   rocket, and for filtering the data coming from different sensors
   including the rocket system and the payload. This research was carried
   out through a competition organized by NASA, which requires students to
   build a whole rocket with constraints on several features and
   performance. The advantage of Credible Autocoding here is the fact that
   this technique is a great model-based (Simulink) approach that uses
   formal method to generate documented code and verifies it.
   We first introduce the different approaches used to generate certifiable
   code, and make a case for the Credible Autocoding framework in Section
   1. Then, in Section 2, we present the project, objectives and
   restrictions under which this research was carried out. In Section 3, we
   discuss the work done on the payload carried by the rocket. Sections 4
   and 5 introduce the rocket equation, the chosen control strategy and its
   stability analysis at model level. Finally, Section 6, the main
   contribution of this paper, describes how to annotate the implementation
   of a sliding mode controller with its stability proof. We discuss how to
   extend the credible autocoding tool to support this more sophisticated
   control strategy.},
