@article{ WOS:A1995TQ01800009,
Author = {Chevalier, R},
Title = {The thirtieth anniversary of `'Diamant''},
Journal = {NOUVELLE REVUE AERONAUTIQUE ASTRONAUTIQUE},
Year = {1995},
Number = {6},
Pages = {55-58},
Month = {NOV-DEC},
Abstract = {Thirty years ago on November 26, 1965, France successfully launched from
   the Hammaguir space base its first satellite, AI, using the brand-new
   `'Diamant'' launcher Eight years after the launch of the first
   satellite, Sputnik, by USSR and seven years after the first American
   satellite, Explorer-1 `'Grapefruit'': France became the world's third
   space power,
   THE ORIGIN OF THE DIAMANT PROJECT
   At the end of 1957, SEREB (Societe pour l'Etude et la Realisation
   d'Engins Balistiques - Ballistic Missile Study and Development Society),
   was created to fulfil the desire of the government to develop a
   ballistic component for the nuclear deterrence force. SEREB gathered a
   highly qualified team which was given the mission to conduct the
   participation of the whole aeronautical industry.
   The first task of SEREB was to propose an action plan to meet the
   objective. Starting nearly from scratch we thought it would be necessary
   to make numerous partial tests within what was called the `'Basic
   Ballistic Programme''. This programme was mostly centered on the
   development of a two-stage missile (1st stage: liquid propellant; 2nd
   stage: powder) with the same range at that planned for the final
   missile: 3,000 km. This would make it possible to develop the necessary
   technologies for propulsion, guidance, piloting, and atmospheric
   reentry. The missiles developed by this programme were called the
   `'gems'': Agate, Topaze, Emeraude, and Saphir.
   As early as the end of 1960, after the delivery of the programme
   proposal to the authorities and its acceptance, SEREB noticed that if
   the reentry vehicle studied for the military missions was replaced by a
   high performance third stage, it would be possible to put a mass of 50
   kg into orbit The launch could take place before the end of 1965.
   This proposal gained ground, particularly within Centre National
   d'Etudes Spatiales (CNES - National Center for Space Studies) which had
   just been created in 1961. In May 1962, an agreement was signed between
   CNES and Delegation Ministerielle a l'Armement (DMA) for the launch of 4
   Diamant rockets by the Army for CNES.
   CNES would participate in the financing of this project to the amount of
   54 million french francs, which was about 10 to 15\% of what it would
   have had to pay without the original ballistic programme.
   SEREB was designated as the prime contractor for the developement of a
   satellite launcher called `'Diamant''.
   CHARACTERISTICS OF THE DIAMANT LAUNCHER
   Height: 19 m
   Mass: 18.5 tons
   Three stages: -1st stage `'Emeraude'', liquid propellant (nitric acid,
   oil of turpentine);
   -2nd stage `'Topaze'', powder stage;
   -3rd stage, powder stage.
   PAYLOAD
   For the first launch, the payload was the Al satellite. This was nor an
   experimental satellite but a simple case, carrying instruments (tracking
   and telemetry) that would allow to verify the launcher performances.
   This small satellite with a mass of 50 kg was protected bu a
   jettisonable fairing made with fiberglass resin laminated material.
   SUCCESS, AND ITS CONSEQUENCES
   After an account of the events that marked the launch programme and a
   description of the orbiting characteristics, the author recalls the
   consequences of this success, particularly the credibility it gave to
   the strategic ballistic component of the French deterrence force.},
